## 12. Methodological attacks

| Area                   | Serious attack / discriminating failure test                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Signal model           | The 150-year linear ramp to exactly +6σ is extremely favorable to detectors responding to eventual level displacement. **Test:** nonlinear subthreshold drift, sigmoid creep, staircase drift, oscillatory secular drift, and drift whose total displacement remains bounded. |
| Null model             | iid `N(0,1)` is far too clean for deep-time cognition. Serial dependence or heavy tails can invalidate both empirical thresholds and the Gaussian e-process guarantee. **Test:** AR(1), Student-t, heteroscedastic, seasonal and regime-switching nulls.                      |
| Calibration            | 200 calibration seeds quantize the tail and reuse one predetermined 1050-year horizon. **Test:** thousands of calibration replicates, confidence intervals, independent calibration/evaluation families, and multiple base horizons.                                          |
| MDR metric             | Ramp displacement increases forever with duration, confounding “slow rate” with eventually enormous absolute departure. **Test:** derivative-only severity, bounded ramps, fixed-total-displacement alternatives, and power curves across horizons.                           |
| Horizon test           | x16 is 16,800 years, but it is still finite and only 40 null trajectories. A detector can “survive x16” by sampling luck. **Test:** far more seeds, dense horizon sweep, confidence intervals, and explicit time-uniform boundaries.                                          |
| Identifiability        | Two channels do not automatically imply independent information; they may share calibration, preprocessing, training data, power, ontology, or causal model. **Test:** inject correlated and common-mode sensor failures.                                                     |
| Reference semantics    | Giving the detector `(blue, green)` assumes away the difficult first-time authorization problem. **Test:** introduce a genuinely novel epoch and require explicit retain→validate→promote→recur transitions.                                                                  |
| Architectural transfer | Scalar Gaussian sky displacement is dramatically simpler than an HTLA/HTNLA/THDE state. **Test:** replay the experiment in the actual Nexus state geometry and real THDE input semantics before proposing architectural change.                                               |

A secondary attack on interpretation is equally important: a detector being statistically good at this benchmark does not make it architecturally appropriate. That boundary is explicitly encoded in the script.

